2026-10-26|Count Islands With Sum Divisible by K

Question:
Given an m × n matrix grid and a positive integer k, return the number of islands whose total value is divisible by k. An island is a group of positive integers connected in four directions.

Approach:

1. Traverse every cell in the grid.
2. Whenever an unvisited cell contains a positive value, start a DFS traversal.
3. During DFS, mark each visited land cell and add its value to the island's total.
4. Explore the four neighboring cells: up, down, left, and right. Ignore cells outside the grid, non-positive cells, and already visited cells.
5. Once the entire island has been explored, check whether its total sum is divisible by k.
6. If total sum modulo k equals zero, increment the answer.
7. Return the final count.

Solution:

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    int m, n;

    long long dfs(vector<vector<int>>& grid, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || r >= m || c < 0 || c >= n ||
            grid[r][c] <= 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        long long sum = grid[r][c];
        grid[r][c] = 0;  // Mark the cell as visited.

        sum += dfs(grid, r - 1, c);
        sum += dfs(grid, r + 1, c);
        sum += dfs(grid, r, c - 1);
        sum += dfs(grid, r, c + 1);

        return sum;
    }

public:
    int countIslands(vector<vector<int>>& grid, int k) {
        m = grid.size();
        n = grid[0].size();

        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] > 0) {
                    long long total = dfs(grid, i, j);

                    if (total % k == 0) {
                        answer++;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time Complexity: O(m × n), because each land cell is visited exactly once.

Space Complexity: O(m × n) in the worst case due to the recursive DFS call stack.

Note: This implementation modifies grid by setting visited land cells to zero. If the original grid must be preserved, use a separate visited matrix.
